(b) Adult cabarets;
(c) Adult motels;
(d) Adult motion picture theaters;
(e) Semi-nude model studios;
(f) Sexual device shops;
(g) Sexual encounter centers.
(Ord. of 5-10-2005, § 3)

238:
(a) The county shall issue a letter of intent to revoke a sexually-oriented business license or a sexually-oriented business employee license, as applicable, if the licensee knowingly violates this article or has knowingly allowed an employee to violate this article and the licensee's license has been suspended within the previous 12-month period.
(b) The county shall issue written intent to revoke a sexually-oriented business license or a sexually-oriented business employee license, as applicable, if:
(1) The licensee has knowingly given false information in the application for the sexually-oriented business license;
(2) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly engaged in or allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances on the premises;
(3) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly engaged in or allowed prostitution on the premises;
(4) The licensee knowingly or recklessly operated the sexually-oriented business during a period of time when the license was finally suspended or revoked; or
(5) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly engaged in or allowed any specified sexual activity to occur in or on the licensed premises;
(6) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly committed a specified criminal activity on or through the licensed premises.
(c) The fact that any relevant conviction is being appealed shall have no effect on the revocation of the license, provided that, if any conviction which serves as a basis of a license revocation is overturned or reversed on appeal, that conviction shall be treated as null and of no effect for revocation purposes.
(d) When, after the notice and hearing procedure described in section 10-41, the board of commissioners revokes a license, the revocation shall continue for one year and the licensee shall not be issued a sexually-oriented business license or sexually-oriented business employee license for one year from the date revocation becomes effective.
(Ord. of 5-10-2005, § 10)

264:
It shall be the duty of every person bitten by an animal to report immediately to the animal control department, giving:
(1) His full name, age and address;
(2) The circumstances surrounding the bite and the extent of the bite;
(3) The medical treatment given; and
(4) The name and address of the owner or custodian of the animal.
(Ord. of 7-13-2004, Art. II, § 1)

271:
It shall be the duty of every person having knowledge of any animal that bites another animal to report immediately to the animal control department:
(1) The existence and description of such animal;
(2) The place where biting incident occurred;
(3) The owner's name; if known; and
(4) A description of animal that was bitten and the owner's name, if known.
(Ord. of 7-13-2004, Art. II, § 8)

302:
(1) It shall be unlawful for an owner of a nuisance animal to permit the animal to roam at large or be unrestrained. The animal may be restrained by a substantial chain or by a leash under the physical control of an adult or in a suitable enclosure that would prevent the animal's escape. The enclosure must provide the animal with the proper shelter from the elements.
(2) A nuisance animal shall be immediately confiscated by an animal services officer or by other law enforcement officer or by another person authorized by the animal services officer if the animal is not maintained in a proper enclosure.
(3) Any animal that has been confiscated under the provisions of this subsection shall be returned to its owner upon the owner's compliance with the following requirements:
(a) Payment of all fees, charges, and costs attributable to the seizure, care and confinement of the animal;
(b) Payment of all costs and fines assessed by any court relative to the animal;
(c) Satisfaction of all conditions, limitations, prohibitions and restrictions relative to the animal as may be imposed by the animal services board or any court;
(d) Execution of a statement by the owner that the conditions giving rise to the seizure of the animal have been remedied and/or will not be repeated; and
(e) Obtaining of any required rabies vaccination and required rabies tags.
(4) If the owner has not complied with the provisions of this article within ten days of the date the animal was confiscated, the animal may be destroyed in an expeditious and humane manner or placed for adoption with another owner.
(Ord. of 7-10-2007, § 8)

